<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Clement IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1190–<a href="/years/1268">1268</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Guy Foulquois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Clement IV (c. 1190–1268), born Guy Foulquois, was a French lawyer and adviser to Louis IX who became pope in 1265. Determined to remove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he invested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, leading to the defeat of Manfred in 1266 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a> in 1268. He also encouraged Roger Bacon's scientific writing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Clement IV" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Véronique PAGNIER · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Ferrande_-_Cl%C3%A9ment_IV_%26_Charles_1er_de_Sicile_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
